<h2>Two timelines, two doctrines</h2>
<p>The economic-offence bail cases make sense once the stage is separated from the standard. Before arrest, the agency&rsquo;s interest in an unimpeded probe is at its height, and <em>Chidambaram</em> supplies the grammar of refusal. After the probe is over, the logic flips: this week&rsquo;s Supreme Court decision in the Rajasthan Jal Jeevan tender case granted regular bail to senior officials precisely because chargesheets were filed, 124 witnesses guaranteed a long trial, and pre-trial custody cannot become punitive. The &ldquo;class apart&rdquo; weighting burns hottest in the investigation window and cools as the case file closes.</p>

<h2>The matrix courts apply at the pre-arrest stage</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Pulls toward protection</th><th>Pulls toward refusal</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Nature of evidence</td><td>Documents already seized; accounts frozen</td><td>Live money trail; undisclosed assets; co-conspirators untraced</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conduct</td><td>Appearances on summons; documents produced</td><td>Evasion, non-appearance, destruction risk</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Role</td><td>Peripheral signatory; professional acting on instructions</td><td>Architect or beneficiary of the scheme</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Stage</td><td>Complaint/chargesheet ready; others not arrested</td><td>Probe at a sensitive, custodial-interrogation stage</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Character of dispute</td><td>Commercial fallout criminalised; parallel civil suit</td><td>Public money, systemic fraud, breach of public trust</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Special-statute overlays</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>PMLA.</strong> The twin conditions of Section 45 colour even anticipatory bail in money-laundering complaints, which is why pre-arrest protection there is rarest of all.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>PC Act.</strong> Corruption FIRs engage the public-servant dimension; courts weigh institutional integrity but also recognise sanction and documentary trails that reduce custodial need.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>GST, customs and company-law prosecutions.</strong> Arrest powers are themselves conditioned; applications often succeed on the argument that the statutory scheme prefers summons-based inquiry to arrest.</div>
</div>

<h2>Drafting the application under Section 482 BNSS</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Lead with conduct, not law.</strong> A chronology of every notice received and honoured is worth more than pages on the presumption of innocence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Meet the custodial-interrogation case head-on.</strong> Identify what remains to be recovered or confronted and show why custody adds nothing — the application fails or succeeds on this paragraph.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Offer calibrated conditions.</strong> Passport deposit, no-contact undertakings, appearance schedules, and cooperation recitals give the court the tools to protect the probe without arrest.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes general principles for information. It is not legal advice; outcomes in economic-offence bail matters turn on the investigation record of each case.</div>
